{"depth_limit": 0, "concurrency_limit": 0, "budget": 0}, "by_type": {"implementer": 1}}, "safety_stops": 0, "is_error": false, "num_turns": 2, "subtype": "success", "api_error_status": null, "result": "The three RCQ chip changes are in, and the three checks pass: `pnpm --dir web test`, `pnpm --dir web exec tsc -b` and `pnpm --dir web lint`. I reviewed the `rcq.ts` diff and re-ran the checks myself. I haven't read the `InputsCard.tsx` or `RcqView.tsx` diffs, and I haven't opened the dashboard to look at the chips.\n\n- **14-day window:** `RcqView.tsx` now reads `{ days: CHIP_WINDOW_DAYS }` with `CHIP_WINDOW_DAYS = 14`. The reset-based window and `useMetagameStatus` are gone, and the share note reads as the spec gives it.\n- **Summed shares:** `expectedOptions` in `rcq.ts` now adds up the share of every row a key has at the chosen tier. A key with one label takes that label as its name, and a key with several is named by the key itself.\n- **Long tail under \"Other\":** `rcq.ts` has `MAIN_SHARE = 0.02` and a new `splitOptions`. In `InputsCard.tsx`, chips at or above 2% show up front, followed by any picked ones, and an `Other (N)` / `Fewer` button reveals the rest.\n\nThe spec says to collect each key's set of labels without saying which rows count, and the implementer collected them from every tier. So a key whose label differs between tiers is named by the key even if only one label exists at the chosen tier. The tests pass either way. Restricting labels to the chosen tier is a one-line change, so say if you want it.\n\nNothing is committed.", "ttft_ms": 1750, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 13341, "uuid": "3e93469d-9e96-441a-86c2-fa1797569aad", "ttft_stream_ms": 947, "time_to_request_ms": 15, "first_content_frame_ms": 947, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 1}
